WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.275 --> 00:00:01.960
YUFENG GUO: Streaming
your data down

00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:05.650
to your own local environment
can be slow and costly.

00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:07.390
In this episode
of AI Adventures,

00:00:07.390 --> 00:00:08.980
we'll see how to
bring your notebook

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:12.599
environment to your data.

00:00:12.599 --> 00:00:14.890
What's the only thing better
than an interactive Python

00:00:14.890 --> 00:00:15.820
notebook?

00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:18.910
Interactive Python notebook
with fast and easy Cloud

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:20.530
connectivity, of course.

00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:22.360
Last episode, we
looked at just how

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:23.791
useful Jupyter notebooks are.

00:00:23.791 --> 00:00:25.540
And if you missed it,
I've included a link

00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:27.160
in the description below.

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:28.630
This time, we'll
see how it can be

00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:31.600
taken even further by
running it in the Cloud

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:33.540
with lots of extra goodies.

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:35.830
We'll run some analysis
with some powerful tools

00:00:35.830 --> 00:00:37.720
and even larger data sets.

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:40.450
As you work with larger and
larger data sets in the cloud,

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:43.620
it starts becoming more and more
unwieldy to interact with it

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:45.310
using your local machine.

00:00:45.310 --> 00:00:47.980
It can get tough to download
statistically representative

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:50.470
samples of the data
to test your code on.

00:00:50.470 --> 00:00:53.170
And streaming the data
to do training locally

00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:55.640
relies on having a
stable connection.

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:58.120
So what is a data
scientist to do?

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:01.420
Well, if you can't bring
your data to your compute,

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:03.910
bring your compute to your data.

00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:05.410
Let's see how you
can run a notebook

00:01:05.410 --> 00:01:09.460
environment in the Cloud
close to your dataset.

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:13.210
Google Cloud Datalab is built
on top of the familiar Jupyter

00:01:13.210 --> 00:01:15.520
notebook with some
added abilities,

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:18.550
including easy authentication
with BigQuery data sets,

00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:23.240
fast operations to Google Cloud
Storage, and SQL query support.

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:25.210
Let's go create a
Datalab notebook

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:28.120
and get set up for running
our notebook in the Cloud.

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:34.960
Install the Datalab component
using gcloud components install

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:36.570
datalab.

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:40.440
Then you'll have a new command
line tool called datalab.

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:44.350
Starting datalab is
a one line command.

00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:46.380
This command spins up
the virtual machine

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:48.510
that you'll use
for your analysis,

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:50.460
configures the
network, and installs

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:52.350
the necessary libraries
that we'll use,

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:56.080
including TensorFlow,
pandas, NumPy, and more.

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:59.166
Once Datalab starts, it will
open up a notebook environment

00:01:59.166 --> 00:02:00.540
that looks pretty
similar to what

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:02.490
we saw in Jupyter notebooks.

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:06.730
However, unlike before,
this is an environment

00:02:06.730 --> 00:02:10.270
that's running on a virtual
machine in the cloud.

00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:13.000
Datalab installs some
samples by default,

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:15.090
which makes a great
place to start exploring.

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:17.280
Let's take a look at the
Hello World notebook,

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:21.250
which is in the Docs Folder.

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:24.590
Here, we can start playing
with the notebook right away,

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:27.760
running the cells
and experimenting.

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:30.520
This is super convenient,
since there was no need

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:33.100
to deal with managing
different Python libraries

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:34.760
and configuring them.

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:36.700
Let's take a look at
a few additional tools

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:37.450
that are built in.

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:44.380
We'll click on the Account
icon in the upper right corner

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:48.340
and take a look at all the
options and information

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:49.690
that we're presented with here.

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:53.740
First, note that the notebook
is running as a service account.

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:55.600
The service account is
already authenticated

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:59.620
with the assets of the
project that we are in,

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:04.090
but if we want to access
resources from another project,

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:07.000
we'll need to grant access
to this service account,

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:08.890
not the user account.

00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:11.470
Since the virtual machine that
the notebook is running on

00:03:11.470 --> 00:03:14.800
is accessible to anyone
who can access the project,

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:17.680
we don't want to expose
our own account credentials

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:20.290
in the Datalab notebook.

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:22.330
Now as we continue
on downward, we

00:03:22.330 --> 00:03:25.420
see that this
Datalab notebook is

00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:29.770
hosted on a virtual machine
on Google Compute Engine.

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:32.560
And the virtual machine
is called AI Adventures.

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:37.870
And we can shut down the VM
by clicking this button here.

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:39.670
Now by default,
Datalab has a feature

00:03:39.670 --> 00:03:42.130
that will shut down your
virtual machine once it's

00:03:42.130 --> 00:03:44.650
been idle for about 30 minutes.

00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:47.800
And you can toggle this
feature by clicking the message

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:50.080
to disable and re-enable it.

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:53.300
This timeout can also be
set to a custom value.

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:57.030
Let's go over to Settings
to see how we can do that.

00:03:57.030 --> 00:03:59.880
The value we set here
will remain in place

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:04.640
across reboots of
the virtual machine.

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:07.960
And if you set it to zero,
auto shutdown will not occur.

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:12.330
So this is just another way
to toggle it off, basically.

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:14.610
And additionally,
you can also select

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:15.970
a theme of light or dark.

00:04:15.970 --> 00:04:18.790
So if you're feeling like you
want the screen to be dark,

00:04:18.790 --> 00:04:22.780
or we can stick with the
light theme keeping it airy.

00:04:22.780 --> 00:04:26.380
Now we have our Datalab
notebook set up,

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:28.340
and we've gotten familiar
with the environment.

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:33.060
So let's see what we can
actually do with Datalab.

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:35.370
Today I'm going to run
through an example that

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:37.920
describes the correlation
between programming

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:40.200
languages used on GitHub.

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:43.230
That is, if you
program in language A,

00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:46.980
how likely are you to also
program in language B?

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:50.040
The notebook is in the Samples
directory under the Docs

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:52.230
that we are in right now.

00:04:52.230 --> 00:04:56.970
And I've also placed a
link to GitHub, directly,

00:04:56.970 --> 00:04:59.640
in the video description.

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:03.930
This notebook uses NumPy and
pandas to analyze BigQuery data

00:05:03.930 --> 00:05:06.360
and visualize it
using Matplotlib.

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:11.450
So naturally, the first step
is to import those libraries.

00:05:11.450 --> 00:05:12.950
The table that we'll
be pulling from

00:05:12.950 --> 00:05:15.830
is the GitHub timeline table.

00:05:15.830 --> 00:05:17.960
And that lives in
the public dataset

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:20.270
samples provided on BigQuery.

00:05:20.270 --> 00:05:24.380
Let's see what the
table schema looks like.

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:26.780
Notice that we can use
this percent percent

00:05:26.780 --> 00:05:29.470
BQ to send operations
to BigQuery

00:05:29.470 --> 00:05:31.780
without needing to do
any additional scripting,

00:05:31.780 --> 00:05:36.740
authentication, or deal
with the REST API response.

00:05:36.740 --> 00:05:39.470
Well, there certainly
is a lot of columns.

00:05:39.470 --> 00:05:42.170
We can also see
how many rows there

00:05:42.170 --> 00:05:46.680
are as soon as I get
past all of the columns.

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:49.159
OK, so if we run
this query here,

00:05:49.159 --> 00:05:51.200
we can pull the number of
rows from the metadata,

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:55.370
and we see we have
about 6.2 million rows.

00:05:55.370 --> 00:06:01.370
And we can also use a sampling
feature from BigQuery,

00:06:01.370 --> 00:06:05.750
and that'll pull rows directly
from the table into Datalab.

00:06:05.750 --> 00:06:07.759
But it's a smaller
chunk of data.

00:06:07.759 --> 00:06:09.800
So you don't need to query
the whole table, which

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:11.180
would otherwise take a while.

00:06:11.180 --> 00:06:13.760
And it allows us to easily
see the format of data

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:15.680
and do some sanity checking.

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:18.650
So we can see here we just
pulled five sample rows,

00:06:18.650 --> 00:06:23.930
and all the different
columns associated with that.

00:06:23.930 --> 00:06:25.560
So we just pulled
these four columns.

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:28.450
So it's really just
a slice of data.

00:06:28.450 --> 00:06:32.560
Now let's go onwards to
the core of our analysis.

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:34.060
These are the queries
that we'll run

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:36.850
to grab the GitHub commit
data and the languages

00:06:36.850 --> 00:06:38.860
associated with them.

00:06:38.860 --> 00:06:42.820
Notice, again, that we're using
this BQ or percent percent BQ

00:06:42.820 --> 00:06:43.810
magic.

00:06:43.810 --> 00:06:46.690
And this time, to
create our SQL query,

00:06:46.690 --> 00:06:50.410
we'll actually be able
to write SQL directly.

00:06:50.410 --> 00:06:53.170
And you notice here that
we get syntax highlighting,

00:06:53.170 --> 00:06:55.900
and it all is
directly in the cell,

00:06:55.900 --> 00:07:00.070
rather than having to wrap it
in the string of an API column.

00:07:00.070 --> 00:07:02.610
Now, these cells
create the query,

00:07:02.610 --> 00:07:05.660
but it doesn't execute them yet.

00:07:05.660 --> 00:07:08.380
To execute them, we'll
actually just call execute,

00:07:08.380 --> 00:07:10.810
and you'll notice here we
have popular languages,

00:07:10.810 --> 00:07:15.850
and that was the name that we
called our first query here,

00:07:15.850 --> 00:07:17.524
name popular languages.

00:07:17.524 --> 00:07:19.315
And so that's where we
construct the query,

00:07:19.315 --> 00:07:21.460
and here we execute the query.

00:07:21.460 --> 00:07:25.510
And looking at the results,
we see that JavaScript, Java,

00:07:25.510 --> 00:07:29.680
and Ruby are some of the
largest number of pushes,

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:34.540
while at the bottom we see
that R, Matlab, and Puppet have

00:07:34.540 --> 00:07:38.520
the fewest number of pushes
in this sample dataset.

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:41.430
We'll also execute one of
the other queries that we had

00:07:41.430 --> 00:07:49.750
and see the number
of pushes by user.

00:07:49.750 --> 00:07:53.751
Anyone see themselves
on this list, perhaps?

00:07:53.751 --> 00:07:56.350
Anyhow, let's load
our query results now

00:07:56.350 --> 00:08:01.110
into pandas data frame.

00:08:01.110 --> 00:08:02.490
Doesn't get easier than this.

00:08:02.490 --> 00:08:04.892
We just call to dataframe.

00:08:04.892 --> 00:08:08.010
And now we can use all
our pandas analysis skills

00:08:08.010 --> 00:08:11.830
on this new dataframe.

00:08:11.830 --> 00:08:14.520
So we can see that we have
the data loaded in DataFrame,

00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:18.024
and we can use that
to scribe here--

00:08:18.024 --> 00:08:19.440
we can see some
interesting stats,

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:22.110
including who has
contributed to the most

00:08:22.110 --> 00:08:25.440
number of distinct languages.

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:27.810
Looks like someone's
actually had contributions

00:08:27.810 --> 00:08:30.320
in nine different languages.

00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:32.970
Pretty impressive.

00:08:32.970 --> 00:08:35.450
Now we will need to actually
further shape our data

00:08:35.450 --> 00:08:38.000
toward our end goal of
correlating languages

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:39.080
with each other.

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:42.080
Specifically, if we want to
have columns for each of the 25

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:47.390
different languages, we
need to have the user names

00:08:47.390 --> 00:08:49.700
and the columns be 25 across.

00:08:49.700 --> 00:08:52.490
So pandas actually has a handy
function to help us do this.

00:08:52.490 --> 00:08:54.330
It's called Pivot.

00:08:54.330 --> 00:09:01.190
And so we'll use that to
create our 25 wide table here

00:09:01.190 --> 00:09:02.870
with all of our users.

00:09:02.870 --> 00:09:04.700
And this is going to
have a lot of zeros.

00:09:04.700 --> 00:09:06.860
Because even the most
ambitious user, as we saw,

00:09:06.860 --> 00:09:11.420
only has nine different
languages pushed to.

00:09:11.420 --> 00:09:13.880
Now we can take this and
compute the correlation

00:09:13.880 --> 00:09:17.370
between the different columns
for each possible language

00:09:17.370 --> 00:09:19.820
pair, so 25 by 25, basically.

00:09:19.820 --> 00:09:23.760
And we'll do that using
the core function.

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:25.974
So we get that correlation.

00:09:25.974 --> 00:09:28.140
And you'll notice that we
have core here the bottom,

00:09:28.140 --> 00:09:29.970
so it prints it out.

00:09:29.970 --> 00:09:33.810
And so now we have
our 25 by 25 table.

00:09:33.810 --> 00:09:37.050
Unfortunately, this table of
decimals is no fun to look at,

00:09:37.050 --> 00:09:39.870
and it's hard to really
tease out any trends here.

00:09:39.870 --> 00:09:42.840
So we'll use Matplotlib to
make a more colorful plot,

00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:45.030
so that we can more
easily see where

00:09:45.030 --> 00:09:47.700
the notable correlations lie.

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:50.810
So once we have that, we
can see we'll run that.

00:09:50.810 --> 00:09:54.110
And wow, that's a lot of colors.

00:09:54.110 --> 00:09:56.840
Let's see here that
the stronger the blue,

00:09:56.840 --> 00:10:00.860
the more positively correlated
the two languages are.

00:10:00.860 --> 00:10:02.570
And the stronger
the red, the more

00:10:02.570 --> 00:10:05.390
negatively correlated
the given language pair.

00:10:05.390 --> 00:10:07.610
There are some
interesting language pairs

00:10:07.610 --> 00:10:09.490
that are notable here.

00:10:09.490 --> 00:10:12.020
First, you see that
Java doesn't really

00:10:12.020 --> 00:10:15.380
mix well with a couple
of different languages.

00:10:15.380 --> 00:10:18.620
You know, here's
our Java, and we

00:10:18.620 --> 00:10:26.480
see that it doesn't show up much
with JavaScript or PHP or Ruby.

00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:29.340
And C and C++, on
the other hand,

00:10:29.340 --> 00:10:31.760
do seem to go
together very well.

00:10:31.760 --> 00:10:33.770
Not super surprising there.

00:10:33.770 --> 00:10:39.080
And CoffeeScript and JavaScript
also get in that blue box

00:10:39.080 --> 00:10:41.830
to be combined together.

00:10:41.830 --> 00:10:44.320
Looking kind of
broadly at the dataset,

00:10:44.320 --> 00:10:51.630
I also noticed that Ruby and PHP
both seem to have a lot of red,

00:10:51.630 --> 00:10:55.210
and some of them quite
red across their rows.

00:10:55.210 --> 00:10:58.330
This makes me wonder if
one of these languages

00:10:58.330 --> 00:11:00.250
could get the prize,
which one would

00:11:00.250 --> 00:11:03.610
get the prize of least likely
to go with another language--

00:11:03.610 --> 00:11:07.190
perhaps something we can
analyze another time.

00:11:07.190 --> 00:11:10.690
So this analysis used only
a small sample of the larger

00:11:10.690 --> 00:11:12.390
GitHub public dataset.

00:11:12.390 --> 00:11:15.250
And if you want to work with
the full GitHub commit history,

00:11:15.250 --> 00:11:17.470
I've included a link
to that public dataset

00:11:17.470 --> 00:11:19.200
down below, as well.

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:22.570
Datalab is a great way to
run Cloud connected notebooks

00:11:22.570 --> 00:11:24.870
close to your data, with
convenient connectors

00:11:24.870 --> 00:11:28.290
to tools like BigQuery
with easy authentication

00:11:28.290 --> 00:11:30.940
to your datasets in the Cloud.

00:11:30.940 --> 00:11:34.150
Thanks for watching this
episode of Cloud AI Adventures.

00:11:34.150 --> 00:11:35.920
Be sure to subscribe
to the channel

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:38.170
so you can catch all the
future episodes right

00:11:38.170 --> 00:11:39.600
when they come out.

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:41.980
For now, go and try
out Datalab and see

00:11:41.980 --> 00:11:45.090
if it's the right
choice for your dataset.

